    "durationMs": 189112,
    "finalText": "The URL Shortener API is complete and fully tested. Summary of what I built:\n\n**Stack**: Spring Boot 3.4.5 on Java 21 with Spring Data JPA (idiomatic persistence), Flyway migrations, and the PostgreSQL driver — all added to the provided pom.xml with the spring-boot-maven-plugin for a runnable fat jar.\n\n**How it works**:\n- `POST /links` — validates the URL is absolute http/https with a host (raw `java.net.URI` parse, URL returned unchanged), validates custom codes against `^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{4,20}$`, generates an 8-char unique code when omitted, returns 201; duplicates return 409 (pre-check plus unique-index safety net)\n- `GET /links/{code}` / `GET /r/{code}` — 200 with `{code, url, clicks}` and 302 redirect respectively; the redirect increments clicks via an atomic `UPDATE ... SET clicks = clicks + 1` so each request counts exactly once; unknown codes return 404 with an `error` field\n- `GET /links` — ascending creation order (by id) as `{items, total}`\n- All error paths (malformed JSON, missing/invalid url, invalid code) return 422 JSON via a `@RestControllerAdvice`\n- `DATABASE_URL` (postgresql:// form) is translated into Spring datasource properties by an `EnvironmentPostProcessor` — no hard-coded credentials; Flyway applies migrations before Tomcat starts serving\n\n**Verified end-to-end**: all 14 POST validation/creation cases, redirect + exact click increments, 404s, listing order, restart persistence (clicks and links survived), second-boot migration skip, and reachability on a non-loopback interface. Dependencies were downloaded and the jar prebuilt during development, so `sh start.sh` just runs it in the foreground on 0.0.0.0:8000 with no network needed. All test servers are stopped and the port is free."
  }
